---
// About - document 03, "/about". Short.
//
// Register split, document 07: the paragraphs describing what Crosspeel is are
// Register D with no byline. The bio paragraph is the one place on the site
// where Tom Hogan's own voice is correct, and it carries his name.
//
// NOTE FOR REVIEW BEFORE G1. The biographical facts in that paragraph - the
// fund, its wind-down, and the month - are not stated in any of the ten
// specification documents. They are drafted from project context and need Tom's
// confirmation before publication. Nothing else on this page depends on them.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="What Crosspeel is, who operates it, and how it is funded."
  width="prose"
>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Crosspeel measures whether independently branded machine-payable API endpoints are actually
      independent.
    </p>
    <p>
      It pays each endpoint, records what comes back, and compares the records. Endpoints that
      share an origin agree on details their operators never thought to vary - the wording of an
      error, the order of the keys, the issuer of the certificate, the way a date is written.
      Where those agreements hold and the prices differ, the finding is published with its
      evidence attached and the operators are invited to dispute it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Crosspeel does not observe ownership, contracts, or intent. It observes responses. What the
      method cannot see is published at <a href="/method">/method</a>, next to what it can.
    </p>

    <h2>Who operates it</h2>
    <p class="bio">
      I ran a long-short equity fund for two years and wound it down in August 2026. It closed up
      on the year and it closed anyway, because the assets under management never reached the size
      where the arithmetic worked. What stayed with me was the data bill. Four vendors, four
      invoices, and no way to tell from the outside how many sources I was actually paying for.
      Crosspeel is that question asked again in a market where the buyer is software, the invoices
      are fractions of a cent, and nobody is reading them at all.
    </p>
    <p class="byline">
      <a href="https://tomhogan.io" rel="noopener">Tom Hogan</a>
    </p>

    <h2>How it is funded</h2>
    <p>
      Crosspeel is funded by <a href="/bakeoff">Bakeoff</a> fees and <a href="/mcp">MCP</a> call
      fees. There is no advertising, no sponsorship, and no paid placement.
    </p>
    <p class="fixed-line">
      No endpoint can pay to be listed, delisted, ranked, or unranked. There is no mechanism to do
      so and there will not be one.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  h1 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-24);
  }

  .byline {
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    line-height: var(--leading-small);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .byline a {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .fixed-line {
    border-top: var(--border);
    border-bottom: var(--border);
    padding: var(--space-16) 0;
  }
</style>
